    "photoName": "Stewed Fruit & Yoghurt Bowl",
    "time": 15,
    "costPP": 10,
    "freezes": false,
    "fridgeDays": 1,
    "feel": "Gestoofde vrugte over cool yoghurt — a Sunday-at-ouma's kind of breakfast.",
    "didYouKnow": "South Africa's dried-fruit story starts in the furnace-hot valleys of the Western Cape, where apricots and peaches were sun-dried on reed racks to survive the year. The Cape even invented its own dried-fruit sweet — mebos, salted and sugared apricot — prized on long sea voyages. Stewing simply reverses the drying: the sun took the water out, your pot puts it back, and every piece comes up plump and syrupy.",
    "ingredients": [
      {
        "n": "dried fruit",
        "pp": 60,
        "u": "g"
      },
      {
        "n": "plain yoghurt",
        "pp": 120,
        "u": "g"
      },
      {
        "n": "water",
        "pp": 100,
        "u": "ml"
      },
      {
        "n": "honey",
        "pp": 8,
        "u": "ml"
      },
      {
        "n": "ground cinnamon",
        "pp": 1,
        "u": "g"
      }
    ],
    "method": [
      "Put the dried fruit, water and ground cinnamon in a small pot. Fruit and spice go in together for a reason: cinnamon that simmers WITH the fruit perfumes every piece from the inside; cinnamon dusted on at the end just sits on top.",
      "Simmer gently, lid off, for about 10 minutes — and stew to plump, not to jam. You want whole pieces sitting in their own syrup, still holding their shape; the moment they start collapsing, the bowl turns into spread. If the pot runs dry before the fruit softens, add a splash more water.",
      "Now the law this bowl lives by: warm fruit, cold yoghurt. Spoon the fruit on while it's still warm, straight over fridge-cold yoghurt — that temperature contrast is the entire pleasure of it. Fully cooled fruit is fine; warm is memorable.",
      "Finish with a small drizzle of honey — but taste first, because stewing has already concentrated the fruit's own sugar."
    ],
    "tip": "Stew a big batch — it keeps 5 days in the fridge and freezes beautifully, so the next four breakfasts are one warm-up away.",
